  <h1>Project Governance</h1>
  <p>
    This page explains how OpenFlows is organized, how decisions are made, and how releases happen — so contributors
    and users know what to expect.
  </p>

  <h2>The architecture governs the process</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows’ own organizing idea — <strong>plan before you build, review before you merge</strong> — extends to how
    the project itself operates. Significant work follows a gated, reviewed path rather than landing as unplanned
    changes.
  </p>

  <h2>How decisions are made</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Small, focused changes</strong> move quickly: open, review, merge.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-trivial features</strong> are discussed and aligned on before implementation, so the approach is agreed before code is written — mirroring the planning gate.</li>
    <li><strong>Security-sensitive work</strong> gets extra care, consistent with the product’s “when in doubt, don’t approve” posture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Releases</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is versioned and released in a controlled way. Releases bundle a single, coherent version of the system,
    and versioning follows semantic versioning so contributors and users can tell at a glance how significant a change
    is. Release notes are tracked in the project’s changelog, which follows a “keep a changelog” style: notable changes
    are grouped by type (added, changed, fixed, and so on), and an unreleased section collects work heading toward the
    next release. Release mechanics are handled through the project’s release tooling and workflows.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="The website ships with the project">
    The documentation website you are reading is part of the OpenFlows project and is published automatically from its
    main branch, so the docs you see are tied to the current state of the codebase.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Roles in the project</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Maintainers</strong> review and merge contributions and steward the architecture.</li>
    <li><strong>Contributors</strong> (that’s you) propose and build improvements.</li>
    <li><strong>Users</strong> surface issues and feature ideas that drive direction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this means for you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read the <a href="/docs/contributing">How to Contribute</a> guide before opening a pull request.</li>
    <li>For anything non-trivial, align early — a short discussion saves a rejected pull request.</li>
    <li>Expect reviews to be rigorous; that’s what keeps the project trustworthy, the same way it keeps the product’s output trustworthy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Community</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, discussions, and announcements happen in the project’s public channels (see
    <a href="/docs/resources/community-and-support">Community &amp; Support</a>). The best place for a feature idea or
    a design question is an issue or discussion thread where the maintainers and community can weigh in.
  </p>

